body:
1
Introduction
Human milk (HM) is the primary source of nutrition for newborns and is essential for their growth and development. The World Health Organization advocates for HM as the sole source of nutrition for infants up to the first six months after birth and recommends continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for at least two years (
World Health Organization (WHO), 2023
). Thanks to its unique composition of nutritional elements, immune and anti-infective factors, and enzymes, HM covers the crucial developmental needs of early life. HM contains various proteins with physiological functions, such as providing nutrition, antibacterial properties, immune regulation, and promoting digestion and absorption. These proteins can be categorized into three main groups based on their composition: casein, whey proteins, and milk fat globule membrane proteins. Whey proteins, which comprise approximately 60 %–80 % of the total protein content in HM, are the most abundant protein component in HM (
Meng et al., 2021
).
Structurally, whey proteins in HM are characterized by small, tightly packed spherical formations with strong hydrating ability and high dispersibility. These proteins are present in HM as high-molecular-weight protein solutions and can easily dissolve in acidic environments, facilitating absorption in infants (
Zhao et al., 2022
). Whey proteins are also rich in essential amino acids, particularly branched-chain amino acids, which play a crucial role in protein regulation, glucose balance, and lipid metabolism (
Wang & Guo, 2013
). Whey proteins have various health benefits, including antioxidative, antimicrobial, anticancer, antitumor, immunomodulatory, gut-barrier protective, and gut health-promoting effects (
Ahmed et al., 2020
;
Guo et al., 2024
).
With the rapid advancements in early-life nutrition, HM continues to be a subject of interest in various research fields. As an indispensable protein component of HM, whey proteins, not only directly influence infant physical development but also play a crucial role in establishing and maturing the infant's immune system. Currently, research on the functions of HM whey protein may be predominantly focused on some well-known aspects, such as common functions like nutrient supply and immunomodulation. Nevertheless, HM whey protein also possesses several potential functions. For instance, its roles in regulating the infant's gut microbiota, in the development of gut cells, and in neurological development may not have been fully explored. Moreover, the dynamic changes of HM whey protein vary due to various external or maternal-related factors. However, currently, there is relatively little research on the complexity of these dynamic changes caused by such differences, making it difficult to establish a dynamic change model of HM whey protein that considers different factors. Consequently, this situation affects the formulation of the most suitable feeding plan for individual infants. Therefore, this review comprehensively investigates the primary composition and functional roles of whey proteins in HM, as well as the factors influencing their profiles. Furthermore, the methods used for detecting HM whey proteins are discussed, as well as distinctions between whey proteins from different milk sources.
2
Main components of HM whey proteins and their functions
The whey-to-casein ratios in HM vary throughout the lactation period. Specifically, the proportion of whey protein diminishes from approximately 90 % during the colostrum stage (0–5 days postpartum) to around 65 % during the transition milk stage (6–15 days postpartum), and subsequently stabilizes at approximately 60 % (
Lönnerdal et al., 2017
). The key components of whey proteins in HM include alpha-lactalbumin (α-lactalbumin), lactoferrin (LF), secretory IgA (sIgA), osteopontin (OPN), lysozyme (Lyz), and growth factors (
Fig. 1
).
Fig. 1
Diagram depicting the effect mechanism of whey proteins on intestinal development. Whey proteins comprise various bioactive proteins that are beneficial for the intestinal development of infants. LF and α-lactalbumin bind with iron and calcium ions, respectively, promoting micronutrient absorption. IgA and IgG can bind to pathogenic bacteria, preventing their adherence to epithelial cells of the intestinal mucosa; IgA promotes beneficial bacterial colonization. LF promotes immune cell proliferation and maturation, and OPN promotes cytokine secretion. In Gram-positive bacteria, LF interacts with lipoteichoic acid; Lyz hydrolyzes the β-1,4-glycosidic bonds in the polysaccharide layer of the cell wall. In Gram-negative bacteria, the positively charged region on the LF N-terminal surface can interact with anionic lipid A of lipopolysaccharide in the bacterial cell wall; Lyz can enter and degrade the internal proteoglycan matrix of the membrane. Apo-LF, LF, EGF and OPN promote intestinal cell proliferation through various pathways. α-La: α-lactalbumin, LF: lactoferrin, Ig: immunoglobulins, Lyz: lysozyme, OPN: osteopontin, EGF: epidermal growth factor, apo-LF: iron-free lactoferrin, LFR: lactoferrin receptor, EGFR: epidermal growth factor receptor.
Fig. 1
2.1
α-Lactalbumin
α-Lactalbumin in HM weighs approximately 14.1 kDa and comprises 123 amino acids and four disulfide bonds. As the predominant protein in HM, α-lactalbumin constitutes 41 % of whey proteins and 28 % of total proteins. Structurally, α-lactalbumin is a major calcium-binding milk protein, with strong calcium-binding sites capable of interacting not only with Ca
2+
but also with other divalent metal ions such as Mg
2+
, Sr
2+
, Zn
2+
, and Cu
2+
, thereby aiding the absorption of essential minerals. Concurrently, the interaction between α-lactalbumin and metal ions alters the intrinsic characteristics of the α-lactalbumin structure, function, and stability (
Permyakov, 2020
). Furthermore, α-lactalbumin contains some essential amino acids that are crucial for meeting the developmental needs of infants. For example, tryptophan in α-lactalbumin can enhance infant sleep and affect neurobehavioral development (
Layman et al., 2018
). Functionally, α-lactalbumin is a component of the lactose synthase complex and acts as a regulator of galactosyltransferase, thereby regulating lactose synthesis (
Permyakov, 2020
). Furthermore, the enzymatic hydrolysates of α-lactalbumin exhibit antibacterial properties and effectively inhibit the biological activity of pathogenic bacteria such as
Escherichia coli
and
Salmonella
, thereby effectively preventing invasive bacterial infections in the gastrointestinal tract of newborns (
Brück et al., 2006
).
2.2
Lactoferrin
LF in HM is a glycoprotein consisting of 711 amino acids with a molecular weight of approximately 80 kDa. Its structure folds into a single poly-β-peptide chain with two globular leaf-shaped structures. The concentration of LF in HM varies with the lactation stage, peaking at over 5 g/L in colostrum and then significantly decreasing to 2–3 g/L in mature milk (
Czosnykowska-Łukacka et al., 2019
). The LF in HM exists either as iron-free LF (apo-LF) or bound to two iron atoms to form iron-bound LF (holo-LF); however, it exists mostly in the iron-free form (
Mikulic et al., 2020
). The ability of LF to bind with iron not only enhances iron absorption in infants (
Mikulic et al., 2020
), but also enables LF to have bacteriostatic effects as free iron serves as an essential nutrient for bacterial growth (
Queiroz et al., 2013
). Additionally, LF interacts with lipoteichoic acid on the surface of Gram-positive bacteria, potentially reducing the negative charge within the cell membrane (
Roșeanu et al., 2010
), which may result in increased cell membrane permeability, thus exerting antimicrobial effects. The antioxidant activity of LF is also related to its iron-binding capacity. Its antioxidant activity may originate from its iron-scavenging ability and inhibitory effect on the formation of iron-catalyzed reactive oxygen species (
Safaeian et al., 2015
). Concurrently, LF can mitigate oxidative stress induced by reactive oxygen species, thereby controlling excessive inflammatory responses (
Gruden & Poklar Ulrih, 2021
).
LF plays a crucial role in the early neurodevelopmental processes of infants. For example, LF possesses anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties, that may reduce levels of brain inflammation in infants and potentially regulate the imbalance between the production and clearance of oxidative substances in the brain, thereby positively influencing brain development (
Schirmbeck et al., 2022
). Furthermore, because a reduction of infections and an increase in iron reserves are positively correlated with brain development (
Merhar & Miller, 2024
), the antibacterial properties of LF and its role in enhancing iron absorption may indirectly support neurodevelopment. Although the molecular mechanisms by which LF improves infant neurodevelopment and cognitive function remain unclear, current animal studies suggest that LF is beneficial. For example,
Chen et al. (2015)
discovered that LF can enhance early neurodevelopment and cognition in postnatal piglets by upregulating the brain-derived neurotrophic factor signaling pathway and increasing polysialic acid expression.
He et al. (2023)
found, through mouse experiments, that LF alleviated cognitive impairment by inhibiting microglial activation and neuroinflammation; specifically, LF improves the composition of the gut microbiota and may reduce neuroinflammation through alterations in the microbiome-gut-brain axis; however, the specific mechanisms require further investigation.
LF promotes intestinal cell proliferation and differentiation. In a cell culture model, LF stimulates mouse crypt cell proliferation, where holo-LF and apo-LF activate the PI3K/Akt signaling pathway but only apo-LF activates the ERK1/2 signaling pathway through the LF receptor (LFR) (
Jiang & Lönnerdal, 2012
).
Jiang and Lönnerdal (2014)
also found that LF activates Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathways, stimulating human intestinal epithelial crypt-like cell proliferation. LF plays a pivotal role in establishing the intestinal barrier, directly preventing the invasion of harmful substances such as lipopolysaccharides, which is crucial for host defense in newborns (
Wang et al., 2021
). LF effectively prevents sepsis and necrotizing enterocolitis in preterm infants and reduces the burden of gastrointestinal and respiratory pathogen-related diseases in young children (
Manzoni et al., 2018
). The immunomodulatory effects of LF can stimulate the synthesis of immune-related cytokines and chemokines in the body, promoting the proliferation and maturation of immune cells (
Siqueiros-Cendón et al., 2014
). Additionally, LF can induce osteoblast development and differentiation, and inhibit osteoblast apoptosis and osteoclast generation (
Cornish et al., 2004
), which is beneficial for bone development in infants.
2.3
Immunoglobulins
HM whey proteins contain diverse immunoglobulins, mainly including IgA (sIgA), IgG, and IgM. IgA and IgG can bind to pathogenic bacteria, preventing their adherence to epithelial cells of the intestinal mucosa; IgA also serves as a substrate for obligate anaerobes, facilitating the colonization of healthy microbiota (
Carr et al., 2021
). Furthermore, IgM inhibits bacteria and viruses within the intestine (
Carr et al., 2021
). Of these immunoglobulins in HM, sIgA is the most abundant, constituting approximately 90 % of the total immunoglobulins (
Kompaneets et al., 2020
). In the stomachs of preterm and full-term infants, sIgA in HM is partially digested. However, the undigested portion retains its biological activity in the gastrointestinal tract, contributing to gut microbiota regulation, resistance to pathogens, and inhibition of excessive immune responses to various exogenous invaders (
Atyeo & Alter, 2021
). sIgA also has preventive effects against various diseases, such as atopic dermatitis, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), intestinal infections (e.g., rotavirus and poliovirus), and necrotizing enterocolitis (
Atyeo & Alter, 2021
;
Hanson & Korotkova, 2002
). Recent studies have demonstrated the presence of an immune response to the COVID-19 virus involving sIgA in HM (
Fox et al., 2020
). Furthermore, sIgA can hydrolyze microRNAs, thereby facilitating infant growth and development (
Kompaneets et al., 2020
).
2.4
Lysozyme
Lyz in HM is an acid-resistant glycoprotein consisting of 130 amino acids with a molecular weight of approximately 15 kDa. Although approximately 75 % of Lyz in HM is bound to casein micelles, its antibacterial activity is not compromised (
Jaeser et al., 2022
). Lyz possesses antimicrobial, antiviral, anti-inflammatory, anticancer, and immune regulatory effects. The antimicrobial effect of Lyz is particularly potent against Gram-positive bacteria as it hydrolyzes the β-1,4-glycosidic bonds in the polysaccharide layer of their cell walls (
Thai & Gregory, 2020
). Moreover, Lyz acts synergistically with LF to sterilize Gram-negative bacteria (
Fig. 1
). In Gram-negative bacteria, the positively charged regions on the LF N-terminal surface can interact with anionic lipid A of lipopolysaccharides in the bacterial cell wall (
Queiroz et al., 2013
). This interaction results in lipopolysaccharide release and cell wall disruption, facilitating Lyz entry and degradation of the internal proteoglycan matrix of the membrane (
Thai & Gregory, 2020
). The lytic activity of Lyz on certain viruses and bacteria can lead to the release of molecules with immune activity, the elevation of glutathione levels in serum and tissue, and the regulation of lymphocyte proliferation (
Delbue et al., 2023
). Lyz may also have antiviral effects through its interaction with nucleic acids (
Bergamo & Sava, 2024
).
2.5
Osteopontin
In recent years, extensive research has been conducted on the role of OPN in HM whey proteins. OPN is highly phosphorylated and O-glycosylated and makes up approximately 2.1 % of the total protein content in HM. Owing to the presence of various proteases in HM, OPN exists either as a full-length protein or in an endogenous polypeptide form (
Sørensen & Christensen, 2023
). OPN is essential for nervous system development, immune system development, and intestinal growth in infants.
In functioning as a cytokine, OPN promotes the type 1 T helper (Th1) immune response, driving Th1 cell responses. OPN can induce Th1 cytokine IL-12 expression, thereby exerting its immune function in the intestine (
Schack et al., 2009
). Moreover, OPN resists partial digestion by gastrointestinal fluids and stimulates human intestinal cell proliferation. OPN upregulates integrin αvβ3 and CD44 expression, thereby activating the ERK and PI3K/Akt signaling pathways and promoting intestinal cell proliferation and differentiation (
Jiang et al., 2023
). Animal model studies have confirmed that OPN stimulates duodenal mucosal growth and nutrient absorption and increases villus thickness and crypt depth (
Jiang & Lönnerdal, 2020
). Additionally, OPN significantly affects gene transcription in intestinal Caco-2 cells (
Christensen et al., 2023
). OPN can also form a complex with LF, binding to LF or OPN receptors on the intestinal epithelial cell membrane. In this complex, the presence of LF increases the anti-digestion capacity of OPN, allowing OPN to reach the small intestine in an intact state, and exerts a similar or stronger effect in the intestine than the individual proteins, with a greater stimulatory effect on intestinal cell proliferation and differentiation than the LF and OPN monomeric proteins, alleviating intestinal barrier injury (
Li et al., 2023
). Furthermore, orally administered human OPN in mouse pups can enter the mouse brain and increase brain OPN levels (
Jiang et al., 2019
). The high concentration of OPN in HM has led to speculation about its potential effects on infant brain development, which could influence cognitive behavior and potentially improve the cognitive function of breastfed infants (
Sørensen & Christensen, 2023
). Therefore, further investigation is required to elucidate the impact of OPN on the developing infant brain.
2.6
Growth factors
Growth factors play an important role in infant growth and development, with vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), epidermal growth factor (EGF), and insulin-like growth factors (IGFs) being the main growth factors in HM whey proteins. The concentrations of these growth factors are highest in colostrum and gradually decrease with lactation. HM growth factors are vital for the development, maturation, and integrity of various organs in newborns, particularly the gastrointestinal tract. EGF serves as a key nutritional factor for normal intestinal cell development, influencing intestinal epithelial growth, maturation, and development. EGF promotes intestinal cell proliferation and differentiation by binding to the EGF receptor (EGFR) and activating the Ras/MAPK and PI3K/AKT signaling pathways (
Tang et al., 2016
).
Beggs et al. (2023)
showed that EGF in HM enhances claudin-2 expression and calcium permeability in intestinal epithelial (Caco-2) cells, thereby fostering calcium absorption in the intestine. The IGFs found in HM includes IGF-I and IGF-II, which primarily bind to IGF-binding proteins (IGFBPs), thereby regulating IGF stability and biological activity (
York et al., 2021
). In the intestine, IGF-I has been suggested to stimulate intestinal stem cell proliferation. Furthermore, IGF protects the developmental integrity of the intestinal epithelium through its proliferative, antiapoptotic, and angiogenesis-promoting effects (
York et al., 2021
).
2.7
Other proteins
Whey proteins include other proteins such as bile salt-stimulated lipase (BSSL), lactoperoxidase (LPO), xanthine oxidase (XO), folate-binding protein (FBP), and vitamin B12-binding protein.
BSSL, a lipolytic enzyme, can hydrolyze various compounds such as cholesteryl esters, triglycerides, diglycerides, monoglycerides, phospholipids, lysophospholipids, and ceramide. BSSL makes up approximately 1 % of the total proteins in HM and plays a crucial role in compensating for the limited ability of pancreatic enzymes to digest dietary fats in early life (
Lindquist & Hernell, 2010
). Studies have shown that certain subtypes of BSSL can bind to dendritic cell (DC)-specific ICAM3-grabbing nonintegrin (DC-SIGN), inhibiting the transfer of HIV-1 to CD4
+
T lymphocytes, which may reduce the risk of HIV infection (
Naarding et al., 2006
).
LPO, a heme-containing glycoprotein, exhibits its highest activity in colostrum and gradually decreases over the lactation period. XO, which is relatively abundant in HM, serves as a source of hydrogen peroxide (H
2
O
2
); HM contains high levels of H
2
O
2
, which possesses antimicrobial properties. LPO can oxidize thiocyanate ions (SCN-) to hypothiocyanite (OSCN-) in the presence of H
2
O
2
, thereby exerting strong antimicrobial activity against various pathogens and spoilage organisms (
Zou et al., 2020
). The combined action of LPO and XO regulates the oral microbiome in breastfed infants and contributes to the development of the innate immune system in the gastrointestinal tract of neonates (
Al-Shehri et al., 2020
).
FBP is a soluble HM protein. It can exist in a glycosylated form that protects it from being hydrolyzed and digested by proteases (
Guo, 2025
). Studies have proposed that FBP may slow the release and absorption of folate in the intestine, thereby increasing the utilization efficiency of body folate (
Lönnerdal, 2004
).
Vitamin B12-binding protein is a glycoprotein that can bind to vitamin B12 and other corrinoids. During early development, infants primarily absorb vitamin B12 through vitamin B12-binding proteins (
Adkins & Lönnerdal, 2002
). In HM, two forms of vitamin B12-binding protein exist: unsaturated and saturated. The degree of saturation determines the intensity of their inhibitory effects against bacteria. Unsaturated vitamin B12-binding proteins in HM can impede the growth of vitamin B12-dependent strains such as
Escherichia coli
and
Bifidobacterium breve
(
Jensen et al., 2011
).
3
Factors affecting whey proteins in HM
Breastfeeding plays an important role in neonatal health and growth. HM composition is subject to various influences, such as maternal diet, lifestyle, physical condition changes, and infant development. These factors collectively shape the nutritional composition of HM, ensuring that it meets the evolving needs of newborns. Variations in the nutrient content and composition of HM can adapt to the needs of infants at various stages, thus offering them optimal nutrition and protection.
The composition of whey proteins, as critical nutritional components of HM, is influenced by various factors, including maternal, infant, temporal, and spatial factors (Table A.1;
Fig. 2
). Studies on the influence of maternal factors on whey protein composition have mainly focused on the mother's physical condition during pregnancy, age at childbirth, mode of delivery, parity, and postpartum weight loss. Infant factors include gestational age, sex, and weight changes. Moreover, the lactation period, geographical location, and living environment also significantly affect HM whey protein content. Understanding these factors and taking appropriate measures to enhance the stability of HM quality and production is of paramount importance for ensuring the newborns' health and growth.
Fig. 2
Factors affecting whey proteins in HM (image created with
BioRender.com
).
Fig. 2
3.1
Maternal factors
3.1.1
Anthropometric indicators
The characteristics of HM are closely linked to the status of its provider, i.e., the lactating mother. Every mother is a unique individual, with variations in age, height, and weight, among other parameters. In recent years, a trend of delayed childbirth has been observed, resulting in decreased childbirth rates in younger women and increased childbirth rates in older women. Maternal age is one of the main factors affecting HM protein content. The total protein concentration in the colostrum of women aged ≥30 is relatively low but increases in mature milk content (
Zhang et al., 2021
). Furthermore, as maternal age rises, OPN concentration in HM also increases (
Zhang et al., 2021
), whereas sIgA concentration decreases (
Groer et al., 2004
).
Maternal obesity during pregnancy poses risks to maternal health and affects HM composition. Maternal body weight reportedly influences HM fat content (
Daniel et al., 2021
). Meanwhile, existing research has indicated that maternal body weight also influences whey protein in HM. For instance, in comparison with normal weight and lean mothers, obese mothers have a considerably higher LF content (
Wu et al., 2024
). Nevertheless, other studies have identified reduced levels of LF and elevated levels of IgM, IgA, and IgG in the HM of obese mothers when compared to that of normal-weight mothers (
Dyndar et al., 2019
). The pattern regarding the impact of maternal body weight on HM whey protein remains ambiguous, necessitating further research for elucidation.
3.1.2
Maternal health status
Maternal health is a crucial factor influencing HM composition as mothers may suffer from various chronic diseases such as mastitis, diabetes, and hypertension during pregnancy and lactation. These diseases can profoundly influence the mother and infant and may cause serious complications. Moreover, these chronic diseases may induce alterations in the nutritional and immune components of HM. For instance, mothers with subclinical mastitis tend to exhibit higher α-lactalbumin, LF, and albumin levels in their milk than normal mothers (
Samuel et al., 2020
). The concentration of sIgA is also significantly higher in colostrum from mothers with allergies than from those without allergies (
Hua et al., 2016
). Furthermore, mothers with gestational diabetes mellitus often have lower sIgA levels in colostrum and mature milk than those with normal blood glucose levels (
Smilowitz et al., 2013
).
Additionally, the mental health of the mother is equally significant. Psychological stress and immune function are linked. Women experience hormonal, physical, and psychological changes during the postpartum period due to their new roles and responsibilities, which could result in psychological distress (
Moirasgenti et al., 2019
). This type of postpartum stress can adversely affect the mother and infant.
Juncker et al. (2022)
found that breastfeeding mothers with high levels of chronic stress had lower HM antibody levels than mothers without stress.
Moirasgenti et al. (2019)
showed that postpartum-specific stress in mothers was linked to a reduced concentration of sIgA in the HM.
3.1.3
Mode of delivery and parity
The mode of delivery is generally divided into vaginal and cesarean sections. Changes in HM protein composition may be impacted by hormonal activity induced by labor pain and uterine contractions (
Dizdar et al., 2014
). The total protein level in the colostrum and OPN level in the mature milk of mothers who undergo cesarean section are lower than those of mothers who undergo vaginal delivery (
Aksan et al., 2021
;
Dizdar et al., 2014
). However, LF concentration in the colostrum of mothers who undergo cesarean section is higher than that in mothers who undergo vaginal delivery at six months postpartum (
Zhang et al., 2021
). In addition, the total antioxidant status in the colostrum of mothers who deliver vaginally surpasses that of mothers who deliver by cesarean section. Glutathione peroxidase levels in colostrum are also found to significantly increase following vaginal delivery (
Şimşek et al., 2015
). The rate of exclusive breastfeeding in mothers who undergo cesarean section is lower than those who undergo vaginal delivery, which can also influence the development of the child (
Chen et al., 2018
). Concerning parity, protein content in the colostrum of primiparous mothers is slightly higher than that in multiparous mothers (
Burianova et al., 2019
), with a similar trend observed for LF content (
Marquis et al., 2003
). Nevertheless, additional research is required to elucidate the impact of parity on HM proteins.
3.1.4
Lifestyle
The nutritional status of a mother directly influences the HM whey protein content. To ensure that babies receive adequate nutrition, mothers should maintain a well-balanced diet with sufficient protein, vitamins, and minerals. A diet rich in meat, cereals, and eggs during lactation can boost the protein, total solid, and energy content of HM (
Bravi et al., 2021
), underscoring the direct link between maternal protein intake and milk protein content. In early pregnancy, mothers with low albumin levels exhibit higher levels of LF in mature milk, and moderate intake of energy, protein, cholesterol, and vitamin A may enhance LF concentration in HM (
Liu et al., 2022
). However, a negative correlation has been observed between protein intake and EGF concentration in HM (
Lu, Jiang, et al., 2018
). Moreover, studies indicate that postpartum vitamin A supplementation can elevate levels of sIgA in colostrum (
Lima et al., 2012
). Furthermore, peptides from bovine whey proteins can be detected in HM from mothers who consume bovine milk during lactation (
Picariello et al., 2019
), illustrating the intricate effects of diet on HM protein content.
Mothers should maintain breast health and ensure stable milk secretion through healthy lifestyle habits, including a good diet, sufficient sleep, and regular exercise. A study emphasized the importance of good sleep, as mothers with better sleep patterns exhibit significantly higher OPN levels in HM than those with poor sleep quality (
Zhou et al., 2022
). Similarly,
Juncker et al. (2023)
discovered a positive association between physical activity and the levels of SARS-CoV-2 specific IgA in HM, alongside IgG antibodies in the serum. Conversely, unhealthy lifestyle habits such as smoking have been linked to reduced milk production and shortened lactation periods. Smoking mothers tend to exhibit lower milk protein content than nonsmoking mothers, and this aspect potentially hampers infant growth and development. Significantly lower OPN levels were observed in HM from mothers who smoked before, during, and after pregnancy (
Aksan et al., 2021
). Factors such as family background, economic status, and educational level also influence maternal lifestyle, indirectly affecting HM proteins. For example,
Zhang et al. (2021)
revealed that higher education levels are related to high α-lactalbumin concentrations in colostrum, total protein concentrations in early mature milk, and OPN concentrations in mature milk. However, sIgA concentration in the HM of low-income mothers is almost three times that of high-income mothers (
Groer et al., 2004
).
3.2
Infant factors
3.2.1
Gestational age
Infants are primarily divided into preterm and full-term infants based on gestational age. Preterm infants, born before full gestation, often require higher nutrient intake due to their insufficient developmental time. Studies focusing on the differences between the HM of preterm and full-term infants have increased in recent years. The true protein content in the HM of mothers of preterm infants is higher than that in the HM of mothers of full-term infants, and the shorter the gestation period, the higher the protein content in HM (
Bauer & Gerss, 2011
). However, protein content in the HM of mothers of preterm infants gradually decreases over the lactation period, eventually reaching levels in the HM of mothers of full-term infants by 10–12 weeks postpartum (
Gidrewicz & Fenton, 2014
). HM from mothers of full-term infants has higher levels of α-lactalbumin and sIgA than HM from mothers of preterm infants (
Garcia-Rodenas et al., 2019
;
Tanaka et al., 2023
).
3.2.2
Infant sex
The sex of the infant can also influence the protein composition of HM. Levels of maternal hormones, particularly progesterone, which vary depending on the sex of the infant, can affect the generation of HM (
Wang et al., 2023
). The infant sex influences colostrum protein content, with mothers of girls often producing HM with higher LF and total protein levels than mothers of boys (
Liu et al., 2019
).
Galante et al. (2021)
observed sex-specific patterns in HM IGF-I content, as well as the influence of maternal social factors on HM content.
3.2.3
Infant weight
Weight gain is a vital indicator of infant growth, often reflecting changes in HM protein content. A positive association exists between HM protein proportion and the body mass index (BMI) of infants at 12 months (
Prentice et al., 2016
). Furthermore, HM consumed by infants with a higher weight gain contains higher IGF-I levels than those with a low weight gain (
Kon et al., 2014
). However, infants should not be excessively overweight, as they are more likely to develop obesity and weight-related complications in adulthood.
Khodabakhshi et al. (2015)
observed that EGF concentration in the HM of mothers of normal-weight infants was higher than that in the HM of mothers of obese infants, suggesting that HM EGF plays a role in the regulation of infant weight.
3.3
Temporal and spatial factors
3.3.1
Lactation period
The lactation period significantly influences HM whey protein characteristics. During lactation, the protein content and composition of HM vary dynamically. During the early lactation period, colostrum has the highest whey protein level and is rich in sIgA, LF, and growth factors, which are essential for the development of the intestinal and immune systems of infants (
Czosnykowska-Łukacka et al., 2019
). However, as lactation progresses, the volume of HM production increases, while whey protein concentration sharply decreases from colostrum to mature milk. The transitional milk stage (6–15 days postpartum) is characterized by declining immunoglobulin and total protein levels but increasing lactose, fat, and total energy content. Transitional milk aids newborns in overcoming physiological weight loss, facilitating a return to birth weight (
Pham et al., 2020
). In mature milk, protein composition remains relatively stable and retains its nutritional value and bioactive properties, with whey proteins constituting approximately 60 % of the total proteins. HM from long-term breastfeeding mothers exhibits higher glutathione peroxidase and catalase activities than that from mothers breastfeeding for 3–6 weeks postpartum (
Sinkiewicz-Darol et al., 2022
). sIgA and IgG levels were found to be significantly higher in HM from the second year of breastfeeding than from the first year of breastfeeding (
Czosnykowska-Łukacka et al., 2020
).
3.3.2
Geographical environment
Studies have demonstrated that regional variations in HM whey protein content and composition are influenced by factors such as geographical location and seasonal climate.
Chen et al. (2022)
observed differences in LF and IgM contents among HM samples collected from Beijing, Luoyang, and Liuyang, with HM collected from women in the southern regions showing higher LF and IgM levels. This finding may be due to the higher temperature and humidity in the south, which foster a more diverse microbial environment in the mother-infant environment, thereby affecting LF and IgM contents in HM.
Yang et al. (2018)
detected regional differences in LF concentration across HM samples from Beijing, Gansu, Guangdong, Guangxi, Heilongjiang, Inner Mongolia, Shandong, Shanghai, Xinjiang, Yunnan, and Zhejiang. For instance, mothers from Guangdong exhibited significantly higher LF levels than mothers from Heilongjiang and Gansu, suggesting a potential influence of climate on LF content in HM.
Geographical disparities significantly influence HM composition.
Munblit et al. (2016)
observed HGF levels in HM from mothers in the UK, Italy, and Russia and found that its levels were lower in HM from Italian mothers. Geographically similar populations have similar living environments, climatic conditions, and dietary habits.
Klein et al. (2018)
collected HM samples from Argentina, Bolivia, Nepal, Namibia, the Philippines, Poland, and the US. They found that populations inhabiting close or similar geographical environments (e.g., agro-pastoralists in Nepal and Namibia) had similar immunoprotein compositions in HM.
4
HM whey protein detection methods
Recent research on HM whey proteins has led to the development of various analytical methods for qualitative and quantitative analyses. These methodologies involve proteomics, high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays (ELISA), and capillary electrophoresis (CE), as summarized in
Fig. 3
Each detection method offers distinct advantages and disadvantages, necessitating careful considerations of factors such as experimental purpose, sample properties, and experimental conditions to select the most suitable approach.
Fig. 3
Comparison of different whey protein detection methods (image created with
BioRender.com
). LC-MS/MS: Liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry, HPLC: High-performance liquid chromatography, ELISA: enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays, CE: capillary electrophoresis.
Fig. 3
4.1
Proteomics technology
Proteomics is defined as the systematic isolation, identification, and characterization of proteins from certain sources. Proteomics technology has transformed cell biology and biochemistry, offering new powerful tools for studying complex protein groups, multiprotein complexes, and posttranslational modifications. Proteomics research employs two main strategies: “Top-down” technology, which involves treating and analyzing proteins in their intact form, and “Bottom-up” technology, in which proteins are extracted from samples and then enzymatically digested into shorter peptide segments for analysis (
Lenz & Dihazi, 2016
). Currently, the “Bottom-up” technology is the predominant method in whey protein research. From a technical perspective, proteomics combines two major techniques: separation and characterization.
Protein separation techniques include sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE), two-dimensional polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (2D-PAGE), and liquid-phase separation. Liquid-phase separation offers superior sensitivity, dynamic range, easier automation, and faster separation efficiency than SDS-PAGE and 2D-PAGE (
O'Donnell et al., 2004
). Liquid chromatography (LC) is the main liquid-phase separation technique used in proteomics. Different types of LC exist, and proteomic analysis often uses two or more types of LC, i.e., multidimensional LC, for separation. Nanoscale liquid chromatography is commonly used in LC-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) proteomics due to its excellent separation capability and efficiency (
Wei, 2018
).
Proteomic analyses primarily employ mass spectrometry (MS) to characterize proteins or peptides. Recent technological advancements in MS, such as Orbitrap MS, have substantially enhanced the resolving power of this technique, almost quadrupling the resolving power (
Wei, 2018
). Integration of S-lens ion optics, advanced active beam guide, and advanced quadrupole technology in the Q Exactive and Fusion series has boosted the sensitivity of MS 1 by over five times and MS 2 by over ten times (
Wei, 2018
). These technological advancements have allowed the accurate determination of the types and levels of various HM whey proteins, laying an important foundation for further exploration into their function and mechanisms.
LC-MS/MS can characterize a diverse array of proteins, which exhibit a wide range of physicochemical properties and molecular weights. Due to its unique advantages, LC-MS/MS has a broad range of applications, enabling the identification and analysis of multiple components within complex samples. Furthermore, its high sensitivity allows for the accurate detection of proteins even at minimal concentrations. Additionally, the structural analysis capabilities of LC-MS/MS are essential for identifying unknown components in complex samples. This technique aids researchers in gaining preliminary insights into the structural characteristics of these novel compounds, thereby providing a foundation for further separation and identification efforts (
Dai & Shen, 2022
). Table A.2 illustrates the application of proteomics techniques to detecting HM whey proteins in recent years. However, due to the high complexity of biological matrices, LC-MS/MS remains an imperfect technique. Analytes detected by LC-MS/MS can be influenced by matrix effects and ion suppression, which may adversely affect analytical performance (
Goff et al., 2020
). In addition, downstream data analysis related to LC-MS/MS remains a critical bottleneck (
Lu et al., 2022
). LC-MS/MS generates data containing thousands of protein signals, and extracting valuable information from this data—such as protein identification, quantification, and metabolic pathway analysis—presents a challenging task requiring operators to possess a solid understanding of bioinformatics.
4.2
High-performance liquid chromatography
HPLC plays a crucial role in the analysis of whey proteins in HM. It is frequently employed for qualitative and quantitative analysis of components within complex mixtures, as well as for preparative purposes. The separation of the sample is based on differences in migration rates through the column arising from different sample partitions between the stationary and mobile phases (
Ghanjaoui, 2020
). This separation capability enables the precise isolation of target proteins from complex mixtures of whey proteins in HM, thereby facilitating accurate quantitative analysis. This provides a reliable foundation for in-depth research on HM components and the development of related products. While HPLC possesses a certain degree of separation capability, it may lack the sensitivity required for detecting trace proteins. This limitation could result in the omission of critical low-abundance protein information when analyzing the complete profile of whey proteins in HM, thereby hindering a comprehensive understanding of the nutritional value and functional characteristics of HM. HPLC techniques commonly used for analyzing HM include hydrophilic interaction chromatography, ion exchange chromatography, reverse phase high-performance liquid chromatography (RP-HPLC), size-exclusion chromatography (SEC), and ultra-performance liquid chromatography (UPLC).
Santos and Ferreira (2007)
utilized RP-HPLC to quantitatively analyze α-lactalbumin in HM and revealed a gradual decrease in its content during lactation.
Yuan et al. (2021)
developed a novel two-step method for quantifying LF in HM. They first extracted LF using phosphate buffer (pH 5.0), purified it with a heparin affinity column, and then detected LF using UPLC at a wavelength of 201 nm. The detection and quantification limits were 1 and 5 mg/L, respectively, indicating that this method could be effectively used to quantify LF in HM. This method has the advantages of simplicity, low detection costs, and higher accuracy over previous HPLC methods.
Oberčkal et al. (2023)
used cation-exchange HPLC on a 0.1 mL weak cation exchange column CIMac™ COOH-0.1 to detect LF in HM. The analysis took approximately 7 min, but this time could be further shortened by using higher flow rates (up to 3 mL/min), and they detected LF concentrations ranging from 2.03 to 5.79 mg/mL in HM samples. The high density of positive charges on the LF N-terminal domain surface results in strong binding of LF to the column, with its elution occurring last during HM analysis. This approach confers high specificity to this method.
4.3
Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay
ELISA, an immunological assay, is based on the specific binding interaction between a target antigen and its corresponding antibodies and facilitates the quantitative analysis of specific proteins (antigens or biomarkers) in biological samples. ELISA offers exceptional sensitivity and specificity, enabling speedy and precise identification of target proteins, thereby ensuring the accuracy of the detection results. The drawbacks of ELISA include the high cost of specific antibodies, susceptibility to temperature variations and non-specific signals, which is one of the primary issues contributing to elevated limits of detection (
Wan et al., 2023
). ELISA comprises four main types: direct, indirect, sandwich, and competitive ELISA. Currently, various commercially available ELISA kits are commonly used to conduct experiments. These kits provide all necessary reagents, simplify sample preparation, require minimal sample size, and offer high accuracy, rendering them widely used.
Olivares et al. (2015)
used ELISA kits to quantify sIgA in the HM of 12 healthy mothers and 12 mothers with celiac disease (CD). They observed a significant decrease in sIgA content in the HM of mothers with CD compared to that of healthy mothers.
Dingess et al. (2021)
developed an ELISA method with high specificity for the quantitative analysis of sIgA in HM. This method employs the two-site “sandwich” technique, with two selected antibodies binding to human sIgA. Validated through MS, this method offers advantages over conventional serum IgA ELISA kits for sIgA detection.
Sha et al. (2020)
assessed the BSSL content in HM using a double-antibody sandwich ELISA technique and found a correlation between BSSL content, maternal BMI, and gestational diabetes mellitus.
Jiang and Lönnerdal (2019)
utilized ELISA kits to determine OPN levels in the HM of 12 healthy mothers over various postpartum periods: 1–14 days and 1, 2, 4, 6, and 12 months. The results unveiled that OPN was most concentrated in colostrum (178.0 ± 17.9 mg/L), with its concentrations gradually decreasing throughout the first year of lactation (48.3 ± 10.2 mg/L at 12 months postpartum).
Ruan et al. (2022)
also employed ELISA to determine OPN content in HM. The results showed average OPN levels of 343.2 ± 163.5 mg/L at 1–14 days, 228.4 ± 121.5 mg/L at 2–4 months, and 204.8 ± 100.6 mg/L at 5–7 months postpartum. OPN content peaked during the first 1–14 days postpartum and then declined. Some differences in the specific levels of OPN were observed between the two studies, but the overall trend remained consistent. Dynamic fluctuations in the concentration of OPN in HM indicate that infants require diverse amounts of OPN for growth and development at different stages of development.
4.4
Capillary electrophoresis
CE is a technique that separates proteins and peptides based on their charge-to-mass ratio, which is achieved by moving charged compounds inside a conductive solution under an applied potential. CE technology offers advantages such as small sample size, multiple separation modes, and high efficiency for protein analysis, with considerable application potential. However, protein adsorption to the capillary wall poses a significant challenge in CE, leading to changes in electroosmotic flow, peak broadening, and reduced separation efficiency. To mitigate this challenge, dynamic or static wall coatings are applied to the inner walls of the capillary, minimizing protein adsorption.
Puerta et al. (2023)
analyzed IgA in HM using capillary gel electrophoresis. They optimized the gel buffer and analyzed two types of IgA in less than 10 min using the short end of the capillary.
Yeh and Tseng (2012)
selectively extracted and enriched Lyz from HM using human serum albumin-modified gold nanoparticles, and analyzed it by CE, determining its concentration in HM to be 1.6 ± 0.2 μM.
4.5
Other detection methods
The diverse and abundant whey proteins in HM play a pivotal role in the growth and development of infants. Aside from the commonly used detection methods discussed above, alternative methods have also demonstrated efficacy. For example,
Zou et al. (2020)
developed a high-throughput fluorescence analytical method to detect LPO activity in human, bovine, goat, and camel milk. This method assesses LPO activity by monitoring the generation of red-fluorescent resorufin from LPO-catalyzed oxidation of Amplex® Red (1-(3,7-dihydroxyphenoxazin-10-yl)ethanone). The determined LPO activity in human, bovine, goat, and camel milk was 20.9, 4542, 1200, and 167 U/L, respectively.
Mukhametova et al. (2023)
employed a noncompetitive fluorescence polarization immunoassay with fluorescently labeled single-domain camelid antibodies to quantify LF content in HM. They obtained LF content values of 2.19 ± 0.02, 2.26 ± 0.14, 1.95 ± 0.05, and 1.85 ± 0.05 mg/mL in four HM samples.
5
Comparison of whey proteins from different milk sources
HM is the primary and optimal source of nutrition for infants. However, when the HM supply cannot satisfy the needs of an infant, alternative infant formula based on milk from other sources can serve as a substitute. Protein compositions vary across different animal milk samples. For example, whey proteins constitute the main proteins in HM, while casein is the main protein in bovine milk. Whey proteins in bovine milk include β-lactoglobulin, which is absent in HM. These distinctions pose challenges for infant formulas to replicate HM. Therefore, understanding variations between animal milk and HM is pivotal for enhancing research and development of infant formulas.
5.1
Bovine milk
Bovine milk is the most abundant and commonly consumed milk (
Xiong et al., 2022
), serving as the primary ingredient in infant formula. Bovine milk consists of approximately 3.2 % protein, with a casein-to-whey proteins ratio of 80:20. Through MS, over 3100 different proteins have been identified in bovine milk (
Maity et al., 2020
), indicating its complexity as a biological fluid. Approximately 70 % of recent studies on bovine milk have focused on whey proteins. The main components of whey proteins in bovine milk encompass α-lactalbumin and β-lactoglobulin, with the latter being the primary component (Table A.3). In contrast, β-lactoglobulin is absent in HM whey proteins. β-Lactoglobulin is also one of the principal allergens in bovine milk. β-Lactoglobulin is a lipid transport protein consisting of 162 amino acid residues, mainly comprises of two variants, A and B, with a molecular weight of 18.2 kDa (
Xie et al., 2024
). β-Lactoglobulin predominantly exists as a dimer comprising two monomeric subunits linked by non-covalent bonds. Each monomer contains two disulfide bonds, formed between residues Cys-106 to Cys-119 and Cys-66 to Cys-160, which are essential for maintaining the structural integrity of β-lactoglobulin (
Liang et al., 2021
). This unique structure also makes β-lactoglobulin resistant to acid and pepsin hydrolysis; intact β-lactoglobulin and its peptides remain in the body after digestion, thereby potentially inducing allergic reactions (
Shao, Zhang, et al., 2020
). The allergenicity of β-lactoglobulin presents an enormous challenge in the development of infant formula that simulates HM. To address this problem, people have employed a diverse range of advanced techniques. Physical techniques such as ultrasound can adequately enhance the characteristics of proteins' form and function (
Wang et al., 2024
).
Shao et al. (2020)
discovered that ultrasound enhanced the hydrolysis of β-lactoglobulin by trypsin, resulting in the production of a greater quantity of small peptides, which in turn improved antioxidant activity and reduced immunogenicity. However, the duration of ultrasound application must also be carefully regulated.
Liang et al. (2021)
demonstrated that short time ultrasound induction leads to the partial unfolding of β-lactoglobulin, whereas long time ultrasound results in the aggregation of β-lactoglobulin. In addition, techniques such as high pressure (
Kurpiewska et al., 2019
) and supercritical CO
2
(
Venkatram et al., 2024
) can also induce alterations in the structure and physicochemical properties of β-lactoglobulin, thereby reducing its allergenicity.
Furthermore,
Yang et al. (2017)
studied the whey proteins in human and bovine milk, uncovering differential expression of proteins linked to immune function and enzyme regulation between the two types of milk. Moreover,
Lu et al. (2018)
observed a higher concentration of proteins involved in the mucosal immune response and complement system in HM, whereas bovine milk exhibited a higher content of bactericidal proteins. Additionally, OPN and BSSL levels were higher in HM than in bovine milk. Therefore, distinct variations in whey proteins between bovine milk and HM serve different biological functions. Various bovine breeds produce milk with significant differences in whey protein content. Holstein-Friesian and Jersey bovines stand out as predominant milk producers globally. While Holstein bovines yield high milk volumes with low protein content, Jersey bovines exhibit the opposite effect. Additionally, some proteins involved in immune responses are more abundant in Jersey whey proteins (
Tacoma et al., 2016
). Physiological factors such as lactation period and parity also influence whey proteins in bovine milk, with the levels of proteins such as OPN, LF, XO, and LPO increasing with the lactation period (
Yang et al., 2020
).
5.2
Goat and sheep milk
Goat milk and sheep milk are utilized as an ingredient in infant formulas and there has been a notable increase in global demand for these products in the dairy market. Goat milk is often referred to as the “king of milk” due to its high nutritional value, digestibility, alkalinity, and buffering capacity (
Saikia et al., 2022
). The digestibility of goat milk is attributed to its higher proportion of whey proteins, which make up approximately 25 % of its total protein content. Goat milk whey proteins primarily contain α-lactalbumin, β-lactoglobulin, serum albumin, and immunoglobulins, which are known for their antibacterial, antitumor, antioxidant, immune regulation, and antihypertensive (
Soloshenko et al., 2020
). Goat milk contains much higher levels of IgG than HM, mainly because the nipple ducts of goats introduce high levels of bacterial and viral antigens, leading to high IgG levels in the mammary glands. Conversely, IgA and IgM levels in HM are higher than those in goat milk (
Park, 2017
). In addition, goat milk contains higher FBP levels but lower folate content (
Zenebe et al., 2014
). Therefore, when using goat milk as an infant formula base, supplementation with appropriate amounts of folate is advantageous to enhance infant absorption. Differences exist between goat, sheep, and bovine milk, mainly due to variations in their reproduction patterns. Goat and sheep milk are predominantly produced by seasonal breeding of ewes, whereas bovines are bred year-round. Studies have shown that goat milk exhibits the highest levels of β-lactoglobulin during winter (
Li et al., 2022
), whereas sheep milk attains its peak protein content during autumn (
González-Ronquillo et al., 2021
). The protein content of sheep milk is significantly higher than that of HM, bovine, and goat milk, with its whey proteins characterized by high β-lactoglobulin and low α-lactalbumin contents. The compositional characteristics of whey proteins are analogous to those of bovine milk, but the β-lactoglobulin content in sheep milk is significantly higher than that in bovine and goat milk (Table A.3).
The protein content in goat and sheep milk was found to be higher than that in HM and bovine milk, but their allergenicity was lower than that of bovine milk. Research has indicated that the structural differences in β-lactoglobulin between bovine milk and goat milk result in varying digestion rates, leading to the absorption of undigested protein fragments from bovine milk, which can trigger more pronounced allergic reactions (
Ma et al., 2024
). Similarly, the digestibility of α-lactalbumin in sheep milk is higher than that of HM, while the digestibility of bovine milk and goat milk is significantly lower than that of HM (
Ge et al., 2024
). Furthermore, sheep milk protein digestion in the infant stomach is similar to that of HM (
Xiao et al., 2023
).
5.3
Other milk sources
In recent years, camel, donkey, and mare milk have gradually gained traction as alternative dairy products. Camel milk has a similar whey protein composition to HM, lacking β-lactoglobulin but abundant in α-lactalbumin, contributing to its low allergenicity. Camel milk is distinguished by the presence of serum albumin, which has a molecular weight of 14.6 kDa and is composed of 123 amino acid residues. This serum albumin differs from that found in bovine and goat milk, which has a molecular weight of 16.2 kDa and is composed of 142 amino acid residues. These characteristics differentiate camel milk from other milk types (
Han et al., 2023
). Camel milk also contains whey acidic proteins, which are absent in bovine or goat milk, further distinguishing camel milk (
Han et al., 2023
). The extensive whey protein profile of camel milk has various health benefits, including antidiabetic, antihypertensive, antioxidant, and anticancer effects (
Hasen, 2024
), leading to growing interest in its consumption. Furthermore, compared to bovine milk, camel milk exhibits a higher digestibility (
Jiang et al., 2024
;
Xiao et al., 2023
) and lower allergenicity. Research has also indicated that camel milk-based infant formula demonstrates enhanced anti-inflammatory properties compared to bovine milk-based infant formula, suggesting that camel milk could serve as a promising alternative for developing infant formulas with additional health benefits (
Mudgil et al., 2022
). Equidae milk, which primarily includes donkey and mare milk, is characterized by lower protein and ash contents relative to other animal milk; however, it has a higher lactose content and similar composition to HM. Compared to bovine milk, mare and donkey milk have low casein/whey protein ratios similar to HM (
Nayak et al., 2020
). This higher whey protein ratio is favorable for digesting milk proteins in infant formula. Mare and donkey milk is rich in Lyz, with concentrations similar to those in HM and surpassing those in bovine milk (Table A.3). Their high Lyz content confers them with antibacterial and antiviral properties. Lyz has been identified as a novel allergen in donkey milk (
Martini et al., 2018
). In addition, the structure of β-lactoglobulin in Equidae milk exists predominantly in monomer form, comprising two molecular forms, β-lactoglobulin I and II, unlike the dimeric structure found in ruminant milk (
Pastuszka et al., 2016
). Moreover, unlike bovine β-lactoglobulin, mare β-lactoglobulin lacks a sulfhydryl group, which significantly impacts protein denaturation and aggregation (
Uniacke-Lowe, 2011
). Furthermore, donkey milk β-lactoglobulin is solely β-lactoglobulin I, making it more readily digestible and susceptible to degradation in the gastrointestinal tract (
Tidona et al., 2014
).
Precisely due to these disparities in components, such as whey protein among different milk sources, exceptional conditions are established for the development of infant formula milk powder catering to diverse special requirements (See
Table 1
.). Whether it pertains to infants and young children with milk allergies or those with specific demands for nutritional constituents, more targeted formula milk powder can be designed and developed in accordance with these differences, thereby providing more precise nutritional support for the healthy growth of infants and young children.
